▸ Context recall

This call decides the context the turn runs on: what to keep of the turns in
hand, plus what to recall from the turn log. The turn then runs on the two
together, in time order.

Current user input:
{{ user_input }}

{{ recall_reply }}

Reply with ONE JSON object holding the fields above, and nothing else. An empty
object means the turns in hand are enough for this turn.

Part 1 — the decision

What to keep of the turns in hand — "context": the turns this input is answered
from. Leave it out (or "keep") for all of them, set it to the turn_ids in the
current context worth keeping for some, or to "clear" for none — an input none
of them carries is answered from memory or from nothing, and every turn kept is
spent from the same window the recall is.

What to recall from the turn log — "recall": leave it out for nothing, or fill it
in for one condition — a period (since / until), a question, or a question within
a period.

A question has two spellings, and either may be omitted.

The "sentences" hold what you want to recall, in your own words, one sentence
per idea — "the turn where we decided the dean of the management school". They
are read by meaning, so they need not share words with the stored turn. Several
sentences are several questions; another question is another chance to be found
and cannot cost a turn an earlier one found.

The "query" holds words that must all be present — names, paths, identifiers, a
command, an error string. It is the fallback when you cannot put the subject
into a sentence, and then it is also read by meaning. "management school dean"
is the shape.

"since" and "until" take a date, or any of: today, yesterday, this week, last
week, this month, last month, this year, last year, "N days ago", "N weeks ago",
"N months ago", "N years ago".

The context is bounded, so a recall might return a truncated selection. Use
"anchor" to guide the drop for a period recall, and bound a query recall with
a narrower period.

Part 2 — the cases

The two fields are decided independently, and each case below takes one value
of each.

1. Keep the turns in hand, recall nothing — the input is answered from what is
in hand.

  input What did we decide about the retries?
    → {}

2. Keep part of them, recall nothing — the plan is in hand, the tool output
around it is not.

  input Let's go with the plan we just settled on
    → {"context": [31, 33]}

3. Keep none of them, recall nothing — the input uses nothing in hand, or says
to drop it.

  input Translate this paragraph into French
    → {"context": "clear"}

4. Keep them and recall a period — a range of time, and no topic.

  input What have I been doing these past two days?
    → {"recall": {"since": "yesterday"}}

5. Keep them and recall a period from its beginning — the oldest turns in it.

  input Where did we start last month?
    → {"recall": {"since": "last month", "anchor": "oldest"}}

6. Keep them and recall the earliest turns of all — no bound to name.

  input Let's look at our earliest conversations
    → {"recall": {"anchor": "oldest"}}

7. Keep part and recall a question — the turn that answered this has left the
context, and the tool dumps around it are dead weight.

  input Who did you say was the dean of the management school?
    → {"context": [27, 28], "recall": {"sentences": ["the dean of the management school"]}}

8. Keep none and recall a question within a period — a topic, and when.

  input What did we say about the anniversary last week?
    → {"context": "clear", "recall": {"sentences": ["what we said about the anniversary"], "since": "last week"}}

9. Keep them and recall a question in a period that has passed — a topic, and
both ends of it.

  input What did we say about the anniversary last month?
    → {"recall": {"sentences": ["what we said about the anniversary"], "since": "last month", "until": "this month"}}

10. Keep them and recall a question for a follow-up — its subject is in the
conversation above, so carry it into the sentence.

  input What about the other one?
    → {"recall": {"sentences": ["the other school"]}}

11. Keep none and recall a question — nothing in hand carries this input, and
the subject is one the turn log may.

  input What about the alumni fund?
    → {"context": "clear", "recall": {"sentences": ["the alumni fund"]}}

12. Keep none and recall the words exactly — a name, a path or an exact string
that no sentence says better.

  input Which turn was about the alumni fund?
    → {"context": "clear", "recall": {"query": "alumni fund"}}
